**How the rule is built** (all numbers are exactly as in the proposal):
- **Timing:** it runs on 1h candles. The entry signal is set on the 15:00 UTC candle, so the buy fills at the 16:00 open. The exit signal is set on the 22:00 UTC candle, so the sell fills at the 23:00 open.
- **Settings:** the stop is the engine''s 1.5% stop, which triggers within the hour. The warm-up is 500 candles, with no take-profit and no cooldown.
- **Missing session hour:** the 15:00 candle only counts if the candle 7 rows earlier opened exactly 7 hours before it. If any hour from 08:00 to 15:00 is missing, there is no signal that day.
- **Volume average:** the 20-day comparison uses calendar days d-20 to d-1, not a fixed number of rows. If any of those 20 days lacks a full session, there is no signal that day. It only looks backwards.

**One behaviour to know about:** the contract forbids signals on candles with zero volume. So if a 22:00 candle has zero volume, no exit signal fires. The position then stays open until the next day''s 22:00 exit, unless the 1.5% stop hits first.

Besides the required checks, the test also confirms that a surge day enters exactly at 15:00 UTC, exits only at 22:00, and gives no signal when a session hour is missing.','2026-11-06T19:17:45.906614+00:00');

All five are intraday, on `15m` or `1h` charts, with every position closed within 24 hours. The trade counts are design estimates.

1. **Round-number stop cascade (15m):** buy the first close through a round price level that hasn''t been touched for 8 hours, aiming for the next level up. About 200 trades a year per pair.
2. **Round-number support bounce (15m):** buy when price falls into a round level and closes back above it, aiming for about +1.0%. About 180 a year per pair.
3. **Noise-area intraday momentum (1h):** buy a close above a band built from the usual move away from the UTC day''s open at that hour, and above the day''s volume-weighted average price (VWAP). Exit when price drops back below either one, or at UTC midnight. About 170 a year per pair.
4. **Low-volume 12-hour selloff reversal (1h):** buy drops of at least 1.5% that happened on below-median volume, with a 12-hour time exit. This is the opposite volume condition to the tested H-050, so I reused that family name. About 150 a year per pair.
5. **Sweep of the 24-hour low, reclaimed within an hour (15m):** a faster, more frequent variant of H-038, also under its family name. About 160 a year per pair.

**Source caveats:**
- **Ideas 1, 2 and 5** rest on Osler''s New York Fed paper on stop-loss and take-profit orders clustering at round numbers. I could only read its abstract page, because the full PDFs wouldn''t open as text.
- **Idea 4** comes from the Bianchi, Babiak and Dickerson paper on volume and liquidity provision in crypto. I read its abstract page and a CXO Advisory summary, not the full paper.
- **Idea 3** comes from a strategy proven on the S&P 500 ETF (SPY), used long-only here. The 14-day lookback is the paper''s value, but the 01:00–19:00 UTC entry window is my own','2026-11-06T19:44:16.285386+00:00');

Sources:
- [SFI WP 24-97](https://www.sfi.ch/de/publications/n-24-97-beat-the-market-an-effective-intraday-momentum-strategy-for-s-p500-etf-spy)
- [Concretum](https://concretumgroup.com/beat-the-market-an-effective-intraday-momentum-strategy-for-sp500-etf-spy/)
- [CXO Advisory](https://www.cxoadvisory.com/momentum-investing/complex-intraday-time-series-momentum-strategy-applied-to-spy/)','2026-11-06T19:52:59.460314+00:00');

Parts of the rule that needed more than buy/sell signals:
- **Band width (sigma):** each bar''s band averages the same hour on exactly 1 to 14 days earlier, looked up by date rather than by counting back 24 bars. That matches the 24, 48, …, 336-bar offsets when the data has no gaps. If any of those 14 bars is missing, sigma is undefined and no entry can fire.
- **One entry per pair per UTC day:** this is enforced in `confirm_trade_entry`, which checks the pair''s trade history, so it also blocks a re-entry after a stop-out. A signal the engine turns down (for example because no slot is free) doesn''t use up the day''s entry.
- **Time exit:** `custom_exit` closes any position still open on a later UTC day at the 00:00 open, so it works even if the last bar of the day had zero volume.
- **Settings:** stop 3% (`stoploss = -0.03`), `startup_candle_count = 360`, no cooldown.

The strategy imports `Trade` from `freqtrade.persistence`. That import is allowed, but the test files didn''t stub it before, so the new test adds a stub with `sys.modules.setdefault`.

The test also covers the per-day limit and the midnight exit. It checks sigma, band and VWAP values against hand-computed ones, and checks that slot 0 and slots 20–23 never enter.

