- **Entry:** at a bar''s close, when the 12-hour return is at or below minus one 12-hour standard deviation (measured on the previous 720 hourly log returns) and at or below −1.5%. The 12-hour volume must also be below the median of the 60 earlier non-overlapping 12-hour volume sums (the prior 30 days).
- **Exits:**
  - sell at the next open once a close gets back to the price from 12 bars before the signal;
  - otherwise sell at the open 12 bars after entry, so no position lasts more than 12 hours;
  - a fixed 5% stop below entry, triggered intrabar.
- **Warm-up: 800 bars.** The longest lookback is 732 bars, rounded up.

The idea gave no cooldown, so the rule says "no cooldown" explicitly. I added no filters, and every number is the idea''s own value, fixed in advance.

The cited paper ranks many pairs against each other at daily and weekly horizons. Using it as a 12-hour rule on a single pair is an extrapolation, and the rationale says so. I expect roughly 100–250 trades per pair per year, which is my own estimate, not a measured figure. Every source in the file is one I actually opened or found.

