How the rule is built:
- **Daily levels:** P, S1 and S2 come from the previous UTC day''s 15m bars. A day is used only if it has all 96 bars, and its levels apply to the next calendar day only. If the previous day is missing or incomplete, the levels are undefined and nothing fires.
- **Entry and exit tracking:** the exits depend on the sweep low and P recorded at entry, and only one entry is allowed per day. So the strategy works out entries and exits with a loop that runs forward through past bars only.
- **Settings:** stop is −3%, `startup_candle_count = 200`, timeframe `15m`, no protections.

Things to know:
1. **One addition beyond the rule:** if the 23:45 bar of the entry day is missing or has zero volume, the time exit fires on the next bar that can trade, on the following day. Without this, a trade could stay open across a data gap.
2. **Mismatch with Freqtrade''s real trades:** the loop counts an entry signal as an open position even if Freqtrade never fills it. It also doesn''t know when the 3% stop has closed a trade. Neither should matter, since there is at most one entry per day and every trade is closed by the end of the day.
3. **Entries can come before candle 200:** on real data the first entry can come at about candle 100, since the rule needs only 100 bars. My first version of the start-up test assumed no entries before candle 200 and failed on the random price series. I changed the test, not the rule. It now checks that no signal fires while the levels or the sweep window are undefined, and keeps the "nothing before candle 200" check only for the hand-built price series.','2026-11-07T12:53:12.418947+00:00');

