The fixed rule, on 1h bars in UTC:
- **Slots:** each day is split into six 4-hour blocks starting at 00, 04, 08, 12, 16 and 20 UTC.
- **Selection:** at the close of the 23:00 bar, each of the next day''s six blocks is scored on the same weekday and block over the previous 26 weeks. The score is the mean return and a t-statistic (mean divided by the standard error of the 26 values).
- **Entry:** buy the block with the highest t-statistic, but only if its mean is at least 0.40% and its t-statistic at least 1.5.
- **Exit and stop:** sell after 4 bars (4 hours), with a fixed 3% stop below entry. At most one entry per day.
- **Warm-up:** 4,368 bars for 26 weeks of history plus up to 24 bars to line up with the UTC day, rounded up to 4,400.

To leave the developer no open choices, I fixed some details the idea did not state:
- A block is skipped if any of its 26 past values is missing or they don''t vary at all.
- Ties go to the earliest block of the day.
- If the top block fails either threshold, there is no trade that day. The rule does not fall back to the next block.
- An entry is skipped if a position is still open. This can happen when the 20:00 block exits at the same open as the next day''s 00:00 entry.

Things to keep in mind:
- **Trade count is a guess.** I estimated about 60 to 200 trades per year per pair without measuring it. That is likely below the owner''s 150 to 500 target.
- **Small edge after costs.** The 0.40% mean threshold is only just above the 0.30% base round trip. Picking the best of six blocks from 26 values may also select noise. The rationale says so.
- **One source.** The only source cited is the Miralles-Quirós paper. I did open its page and checked its summary. The idea''s Kaiko evidence on ETF-hour volume is marked in the rationale as reported by the scout, because I did not ope','2026-11-07T14:45:46.081339+00:00');

