The rule is as written: 1h, 3% stop, a 4-candle cooldown, `startup_candle_count = 1000`, and the 720-bar ATRp median is shifted one bar so it leaves out the current candle. Some design choices you should know about:

- **Exits run in `custom_exit`, not as an `exit_long` signal.** Each trade has to exit on its own rule based on its entry tag (''trend'' on EMA10 < EMA30, ''range'' on RSI14 ≥ 50), and a plain exit signal in Freqtrade would close any trade whatever its tag. So `populate_exit_trend` stores the two exit conditions in columns, and `custom_exit` reads them from the last closed candle. The 24-hour exit also lives there: it fires at the open 24 hours after entry.
- **The indicators are written in pandas/numpy instead of calling TA-Lib.** The test suite replaces TA-Lib with a stub that only has SMA and RSI, so there was no ADX, DI, ATR or EMA to call. ADX, ±DI, ATR and RSI use Wilder smoothing, and the EMAs start from a simple average of the first values, the way TA-Lib does it. The first values may differ slightly from TA-Lib''s, but that difference is gone well before the 1000-candle warm-up ends.
- **RSI with no price movement at all is treated as undefined**, so it gives no signal. If both directional lines (+DI and −DI) are zero, the trend-strength input to ADX is taken as 0 so ADX keeps computing.

One gap in the test: `test_signals_binary_and_both_tags_present` checks only that every tag is ''trend'' or ''range''. Despite its name, it does not check that both tags actually show up in the synthetic data.','2026-11-07T15:26:43.088563+00:00');

