The strategy runs on 15m candles with a 3% stop and `startup_candle_count = 96`. Each candle''s UTC time is converted to New York time, so daylight saving is handled per date. The 09:30 ET bar''s high and low are carried forward through the same New York day only, never backwards. The entry is the first close above the range high from 09:45 to 14:30 ET on an armed weekday. A day is armed when the range is 0.3–2.5% of its low.

Two places where the code had to choose how to express the rule:
- **The time exit doesn''t know when the trade was opened.** Exit signals are worked out per candle without that information, so "any bar at or after 15:45 ET on the entry date, or on any later date" is written as: any bar opening at or after 15:45 ET, before 09:45 ET, or on a weekend. That gives the same result. A trade can only open after a 09:45 ET or later bar closes, so a bar before 09:45 ET or on a weekend always falls on a later date than the entry. The cost is that exit signals also appear overnight while no trade is open, where the day''s range isn''t defined yet. Those signals use only the clock and are ignored when there''s no position, but a check that rejects any exit on a row with an empty range value would flag them.
- **A zero-volume first breakout cancels the day.** If the first bar that closes above the range high has zero volume, there''s no entry that day. A later breakout doesn''t count, because the rule allows only the first one.','2026-11-07T17:13:48.992707+00:00');

1. **NY close near 10-day high, overnight hold (1h):** buy at 16:00 New York time when price is within 3% of the 10-day high, sell at 10:00 New York the next morning. It is adapted from a Quantpedia article on Bitcoin''s overnight sessions. The source buys only at the exact high and holds Friday to Monday, which is over 24 hours, so I loosened the entry to reach the trade count and made every night a separate 18-hour hold.
2. **Negative realized skewness (15m):** at 00:00 UTC, buy if the previous day''s 15m returns were strongly negatively skewed, and sell before the next 00:00. The paper''s abstract says skewness predicts returns but not in which direction. I took the direction from Amaya et al. on stocks and from Lee and Wang (2025) on crypto.
3. **Coin-specific drop against a flat BTC (1h):** buy when a coin falls at least 2% (and 2.5 sigma) relative to BTC over 6 hours while BTC itself is flat. Exit when 60% of the gap closes, on a 4% stop, or after 12 hours. BTC/USDT uses ETH as its reference. A practitioner audit I cite warns that this kind of reversion is weak in liquid coins, which is why the thresholds are large.
4. **24-hour momentum with a trailing stop (1h):** buy a 24-hour move of at least 2% and 1.5 sigma, with a 2.5-ATR stop that only moves up and a 24-hour time exit. It adapts a 6h-bar arXiv preprint. The source re-tunes its lookback and threshold every month, so here they are fixed choices rather than published values.

All four are marked as not using published fixed parameters, and the trade counts (150–170 a year per pair) are my design estimates. Every source licence is listed as "unknown".

